# Copy to .env.local and fill in. There are only three, and leaving the last
# one out is the one that breaks in a way you will not see locally.

# --- Where the browser sends API calls --------------------------------------
# Keep this as /api. The browser then talks to this same site, Next forwards
# /api/* to the backend itself (see the rewrite in next.config.ts), and the
# session cookie is first-party the whole way. Pointing it straight at the
# backend's own hostname makes the cookie cross-site, which Safari and Chrome
# both drop by default - you would log in and immediately be a stranger again.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=/api

# --- Where Next forwards those calls (server side, never shipped to the browser)
# Local: the backend on your machine. Production: the backend's real URL, for
# example https://your-api.onrender.com
API_PROXY_URL=http://localhost:5000

# --- This site's own public address (required in production) ----------------
# Used to turn relative image paths into absolute URLs for link previews.
# When somebody pastes a story into WhatsApp, WhatsApp fetches the page from
# the open internet and has no idea what "/uploads/cover.jpg" points at. Leave
# this unset and every shared link advertises http://localhost:3000, which
# nobody else can reach - and it looks fine on your own machine, which is why
# it goes unnoticed.
#
# Set it to the real origin, no trailing slash: https://blogs.yourdomain.in
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
